-- Passage rg4-evidence-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('50bc9294-13a1-5d28-938c-4282fa71753e', 'rg4-evidence-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-evidence-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Shape of a Snowflake$q$, $q$Every snowflake begins high up in a cloud. The air there is very cold. A tiny bit of dust or pollen floats in the cloud. Water vapor, which is water in the form of a gas, freezes onto this speck. A small ice crystal begins to form.

As the crystal falls, more water vapor freezes onto it. The crystal grows arms. Almost every snowflake has six sides or six arms. This happens because of the way water freezes into ice.

The shape of each arm depends on the air the crystal falls through. Colder or wetter air can give the arms a different pattern. Two crystals hardly ever travel through exactly the same air. That is why no two snowflakes look exactly alike.

Scientists study snowflakes by catching them on dark cloth. They look at them through a magnifying lens before they melt. Some people even take close-up photos. By looking at a snowflake's shape, a scientist can learn about the air high above the ground.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
