-- Passage rg4-figurative-language-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('dd336eed-36a2-5926-b347-1e1ed06c54c6', 'rg4-figurative-language-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Grandpa Teo's Clay$q$, $q$Every Saturday, Mia visited her grandfather Teo in his pottery studio. The room smelled like wet earth. Shelves were lined with bowls, cups, and vases in every color.

"Today you will make your first bowl," Grandpa Teo said. He dropped a lump of gray clay onto the spinning wheel. Mia pressed her hands against it. The clay wiggled and wobbled. In a few seconds, it flopped over like a sleepy puppy.

Mia frowned. "I ruined it," she said.

Grandpa Teo chuckled. "Don't cry over spilled milk. We will simply start again." He showed her how to keep her hands wet and her elbows tucked against her knees.

Mia tried again. This time she held her hands as still as stones. Slowly, the clay rose up into a small, round shape. It was not perfect. One side was a little thicker than the other. Still, it was a bowl.

"You have a knack for this," Grandpa Teo said.

A week later, the bowl came out of the hot kiln. Mia had painted it the color of the ocean. It was hard and shiny now, not soft and floppy. She decided to give it to her mother. Grandpa Teo smiled. "Practice makes perfect," he said, "and you are off to a fine start."$q$, 'draft')
