   $q$In paragraph 3, the batteries "save energy for a rainy day." What does this idiom mean here?$q$, $q$[{"text":"They use up all the energy right away.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"They only work on days when it is raining.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"They keep energy to use later when it is needed.","is_correct":true},{"text":"They catch rainwater to make electricity.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$They keep energy to use later when it is needed.$q$, $q$The sentence says the saved energy keeps "the lights stay on after dark," when the panels make no power. Saving for a rainy day means saving for a time of need, not only for actual rain.$q$, 'draft', '51875581-56e4-51ee-b6f8-322c64214379')
